---
id: 91a9431a-146f-48a7-ab9c-dc499df42e6f
title: Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia
---

# Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia

- **Definition** - inherited disorder in enzymes of the cortisol biosynthetic pathway resulting in <u>insufficiency of hormones downstream of the block</u>, w/ impaired -ve feedback and <u>increased ACTH secretion</u>
- **Pathophysiology of CAH:**
  - **Principles of pathophysiology of CAH:**
    - <u>Inherited defect of an enzyme in the cortisol biosynthetic pathway</u> - most commonly a 21-hydroxylase deficiency, which results in **insufficiency of hormones downstream of the genetic defect**
    - <u>Impaired negative feedback</u> - increased ACTH secretion as a result of downstream hormone insufficiency, resulting in **adrenal hyperplasia**, and **production of steroids upstream of the enzyme block**
  - **Forms of CAH:**
    - <u>21-hydroxylase deficiency</u> - impaired synthesis of cortisol and aldosterone, and subsequent accumulation of 17OH progesterone, which is subsequently converted to androgens (timing of presentation dependent on severity of defect)
    - <u>17-hydroxylase deficiency and 11-hydroxylase deficiency</u> - increased production of 11-deoxycorticosterone which has mineralcorticoid activity (manifests as young hypertension)
- **Clinical features of CAH** - attributed to 1) insufficiency of steroid hormones downstream of the block, and 2) excessive hormones upstream of the enzyme block:
  - **Presentation of 21-hydroxylase deficiency** - related to deficiency of cortisol, aldosterone, and accumulation of androgens, and <u>severity of enzyme defect</u>:
    - <u>Severe CAH</u> - presentation as Addisonian crisis during infancy due to glucocorticoid and minerocorticoid deficiency, and ambiguous genitalia in girls
    - <u>Mild-to-moderate CAH</u> - presentation w/ cortisol insufficiency, and androgen excess as pseudopuberty, amenorrhoea/ hirsutism
  - **Presentation of 17- or 11-hydroxylase deficiency** - presents as hypertension and a picture of minerocorticoid excess (e.g. hypoK, metabolic alkalosis)
- **Ix:**
  - <u>Circulating 17OH progesterone levels</u> - raised in 21-hydroxylase activity (may only be demonstrated after ACTH administration in late-onset cases)
- **Mx:**
  - **Principles of Mx** - replace deficient corticosteroids and to suppress ACTH-driven adrenal androgen production:
    - <u>Corticosteroid replacement</u> - balance between adequate suppression of adrenal androgen excess and iatrogenic Cushing's syndrome (titrated against growth velocity in children)
    - <u>Mx of hirsutism</u> - role of corticosteroid replacement is debatable, and women w/ late-onset 21-hydroxylase activity may benefit from sole anti-androgen therapy
